            <h3>Before Your Visit (Preparation Checklist):</h3>
            <div class="checklist">
                <h4>Preparation Checklist</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Fast for 10-12 hours (water allowed, no food, coffee, or gum)</li>
                    <li>Avoid alcohol for 48 hours before</li>
                    <li>No strenuous exercise 24 hours prior</li>
                    <li>Bring previous medical records and imaging CDs</li>
                    <li>List all current medications and supplements</li>
                    <li>Women: Schedule outside menstrual period (day 3-10 ideal)</li>
                    <li>Wear comfortable, easily removable clothing (no metal)</li>
                    <li>Leave valuables at hotel</li>
                    <li>Bring passport for registration</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h3>Day-of Process Timeline:</h3>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>7:30 AM - Registration (30 min):</strong> International desk check-in, medical history questionnaire, wristband placement</li>
                <li><strong>8:00 AM - Blood Draw (15 min):</strong> Fasting blood panel, multiple tubes collected, hydration break offered</li>
                <li><strong>8:30 AM - Imaging Block (90 min):</strong>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Chest X-ray or CT</li>
                        <li>Abdominal ultrasound</li>
                        <li>Thyroid ultrasound (if included)</li>
                        <li>Cardiac ultrasound/ECHO (if included)</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li><strong>10:00 AM - Breakfast Break:</strong> Hospitals provide healthy breakfast for fasting patients</li>
                <li><strong>10:30 AM - Functional Tests (45 min):</strong> ECG, pulmonary function, bone density, vision, hearing</li>
                <li><strong>11:15 AM - Specialist Consultations (60 min):</strong> Physical exam, discussion of symptoms, risk factor review</li>
                <li><strong>12:15 PM - Endoscopy Slot (if selected):</strong> Gastroscopy/colonoscopy with sedation (2-3 hours including recovery)</li>
                <li><strong>2:00 PM - Wrap-up:</strong> Discharge instructions, report timeline, follow-up scheduling</li>
            </ol>

            <h3>Timeline by Package Type:</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Basic Package:</strong> 4-5 hours, same-day results for lab/imaging</li>
                <li><strong>Comprehensive Package:</strong> 6-8 hours, preliminary results same day, final report in 3-5 days</li>
                <li><strong>VIP Package with Endoscopy:</strong> Full day (2 sessions if combined), comprehensive report in 5-7 days</li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Section 9: Risks and Important Considerations -->
            <h2>9. Risks and Important Considerations</h2>

            <div class="risk-box">
                <h3>⚠️ Medical Risks to Understand</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Radiation Exposure:</strong> CT scans involve ionizing radiation. A chest CT delivers ~2-3 mSv (equivalent to 1 year of natural background radiation). Discuss whether low-dose protocols are used.</li>
                    <li><strong>False Positives:</strong> Tumor markers can elevate due to benign conditions. CA19-9 rises with gallstones; CEA with smoking. Always confirm with imaging or biopsy.</li>
                    <li><strong>Incidental Findings:</strong> 15-20% of CT/MRI scans reveal unexpected findings requiring further investigation. This can cause anxiety and additional costs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Endoscopy Risks:</strong> Perforation risk is very low (1:10,000) but real. Bleeding can occur after polypectomy. Sedation risks for patients with severe cardiac/pulmonary disease.</li>
                    <li><strong>Overdiagnosis:</strong> Some cancers found on screening (especially thyroid, prostate) may be indolent and never require treatment. This is the "thyroid cancer epidemic" dilemma.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h3>⚡ Practical Considerations for International Patients</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Language:</strong> Verify English proficiency at international desk. For complex packages, consider hiring a medical interpreter ($100-200/day)</li>
                <li><strong>Medical Records:</strong> Request English reports. Ask for DICOM digital imaging files to share with home physicians.</li>
                <li><strong>Follow-up Plans:</strong> Discuss any findings with your home doctor before travel. Establish a local follow-up protocol.</li>
                <li><strong>Insurance:</strong> Confirm whether your insurer covers international preventive care. Save all receipts for reimbursement.</li>
                <li><strong>Timeline:</strong> Allow 3-7 days in China for comprehensive screening. Some results require pathology processing time.</li>
                <li><strong>Continuity of Care:</strong> China hospitals do not replace ongoing care. Establish a relationship with a local physician for continuity.</li>
                <li><strong>Payment:</strong> Most hospitals require upfront payment. International credit cards accepted but expect 2-3% foreign transaction fee.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Who Should Consider VIP/Comprehensive Packages?</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Age 45+ with no recent comprehensive checkup</li>
                <li>Family history of cancer, cardiovascular disease, or diabetes</li>
                <li>Personal history of smoking (past or present)</li>
                <li>Symptoms: unexplained weight loss, fatigue, pain, or bleeding</li>
                <li>Occupational exposure to toxins or carcinogens</li>
                <li>Those seeking peace of mind about current health status</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Who Might Prefer Basic Screening?</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Young adults (25-40) with no symptoms or family history</li>
                <li>Those with recent checkups at home (within 2 years)</li>
                <li>Limited budget prioritizing specific concerns</li>
                <li>First-tier screening for asymptomatic individuals</li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Section 10: Conclusion and CTA -->
            <h2>10. Conclusion: Your Next Step to Proactive Health</h2>

            <p>China's top hospitals offer <strong>world-class preventive healthcare</strong> at accessible price points. Whether you choose a basic metabolic panel or a comprehensive VIP executive screening, you're accessing the same Grade 3A institutions that serve Chinese leadership—with the added benefit of cutting-edge technology, high patient volume experience, and significant cost savings.</p>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <h3>💡 Key Takeaways</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Save 60-75%</strong> compared to US/European screening costs</li>
                    <li><strong>Choose NMPA-accredited</strong> Grade 3A hospitals for quality assurance</li>
                    <li><strong>Match package to risk:</strong> Family history of cancer = comprehensive VIP with endoscopy</li>
                    <li><strong>Plan ahead:</strong> Book executive packages 2-4 weeks in advance</li>
                    <li><strong>Bring records:</strong> Previous labs and imaging dramatically improve interpretation</li>
                    <li><strong>Budget for follow-up:</strong> 40% of executive checkups identify actionable findings</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cta-box">
                <h3>Ready to Prioritize Your Health?</h3>
                <p>Let us help you arrange a comprehensive health checkup at China's top hospitals. We assist with hospital selection, appointment booking, interpretation services, and travel coordination.</p>
                <a href="../index.html#contact" class="cta-button">Get Free Consultation</a>
